00:31:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on a key nuance—and you’re right that **modern game engines like Unreal and Unity handle the vast majority of platform-specific code behind the scenes.** For a game built entirely within such an engine, using only its cross-platform features, recompilation for a different platform often *does* work with minimal source code changes. So why isn’t it always just a recompile? The answer lies in what the engine *doesn’t* abstract and what developers add themselves: ### What the engine abstracts - Graphics APIs (DirectX, Vulkan, Metal, console APIs) – the engine translates your visual logic into the appropriate low-level API. - Input handling – keyboard, mouse, controller, touch. - Audio, file I/O, windowing. - Memory management, threading, and common utilities. ### What still requires work even with an engine 1. **Project setup and build configuration** – You must tell the engine what platform you’re targeting (e.g., set up a new build configuration for PlayStation, link Sony’s SDK libraries). This isn’t a code change, but it’s a necessary manual step. 2. **Platform-specific features** – Many games use unique platform capabilities that the engine provides but requires explicit setup: - Nintendo Switch gyroscope, HD Rumble, or touch screen. - PlayStation DualSense adaptive triggers and haptics. - Xbox Smart Delivery, quick resume support. - PC support for ultrawide monitors, ray tracing, or modding. - Online services (PSN, Xbox Live, Epic Online Services) which each have their own SDK. 3. **Performance and optimization** – Even if the code compiles, a game that runs at 60 FPS on PlayStation may run poorly on Switch due to different hardware limits. Developers often need to adjust graphics settings, asset quality, draw distances, or even rewrite certain systems (e.g., physics or streaming) to fit the new platform’s constraints. This is the biggest hidden effort. 4. **Certification and legal requirements** – Each platform holder requires passing certification tests (e.g., Sony’s TRC, Microsoft’s XR, Nintendo’s LOT). This involves fixing bugs, adding achievements, compliance with controller mappings, screen resolution standards, etc. These are often trivial but still require code changes. 5. **Custom engine modifications or plugins** – Many studios customize the engine (e.g., for their own rendering pipeline, networking, or asset streaming). Those custom parts may not be cross-platform and need rewriting. ### In practice - **A pure engine game** (e.g., a simple 2D platformer built entirely with Unreal’s blueprint system and no custom C++) can often be recompiled for another platform with very few code changes—maybe just a few configuration tweaks and some asset adjustments for performance. - **A AAA game** (e.g., *Fortnite*, *Gears of War*) uses the engine heavily but also has massive amounts of custom engine modifications, complex online systems, and platform-specific optimizations. Porting them still requires months of work, not just a recompile. ### Summary You’re not wrong: **if the game is built on an engine that fully abstracts platform differences, and the developer never touches platform-specific code, then a recompile is essentially all that’s needed** (ignoring trivial setup steps). But in reality, most commercial games go far beyond the engine’s abstracted layer, leading to the misconception that porting is always a big rewrite. It’s more accurate to say: **the engine handles the common 90%, but the final 10%—and the performance tuning—is what makes porting a real engineering effort.**
